<h3 id="code-the-vgg-block-pattern">Code: the VGG block pattern</h3>
<pre><code class="language-python">import torch.nn as nn

def vgg_block(in_ch, out_ch, n_convs):
    layers = []
    for _ in range(n_convs):
        layers += [nn.Conv2d(in_ch, out_ch, kernel_size=3, padding=1),
                   nn.ReLU(inplace=True)]
        in_ch = out_ch
    layers += [nn.MaxPool2d(kernel_size=2, stride=2)]
    return nn.Sequential(*layers)

# VGG-16's convolutional stages: (64)x2, (128)x2, (256)x3, (512)x3, (512)x3
features = nn.Sequential(
    vgg_block(3, 64, 2), vgg_block(64, 128, 2), vgg_block(128, 256, 3),
    vgg_block(256, 512, 3), vgg_block(512, 512, 3),
)
x = torch.randn(1, 3, 224, 224)
print(features(x).shape)  # torch.Size([1, 512, 7, 7])
</code></pre>
<p>Five poolings halve 224 → 112 → 56 → 28 → 14 → 7. That 7×7×512 tensor is the classic "conv feature volume" that older papers fed to SVMs or FC layers — and that you'll feed to global average pooling in modern variants.</p>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>For your research.</strong> Cite these three papers correctly (references at the end of this book) — mis-citing AlexNet's venue or year is a classic student tell. More substantially: when you write "we use a VGG-style backbone," say what you mean — stacked 3×3 convolutions with doubling channels after each pooling (64→128→256→512). That channel-doubling convention (halve spatial, double channels) preserves approximate computational cost per stage and is the single most-copied design decision in CNN history. If you deviate from it, explain why.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 id="engineering-details-the-history-books-skip">Engineering details the history books skip</h3>
<p><strong>AlexNet's forgotten tricks.</strong> Two deserve mention because they teach lessons. (a) <strong>Local Response Normalization (LRN)</strong> — AlexNet normalized each activation by its neighbors' activity, inspired by lateral inhibition in real neurons. It helped slightly in 2012 and was abandoned within two years once Batch Normalization (2015) did the job properly. Lesson: biologically-inspired heuristics lose to principled optimization tools; don't cargo-cult old tricks into new architectures. (b) <strong>Split-GPU training</strong> — the model was split across two GTX 580s because one card couldn't hold it. The accidental consequence: the two halves learned somewhat specialized features (one largely color-agnostic, one color-focused) — an early hint of the grouped convolutions that ResNeXt later used deliberately. Engineering constraints becoming design principles is a recurring pattern in this field.</p>
<p><strong>Where VGG's 138 million parameters actually live.</str